WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.378
Here is a simple way to compare two paper-plane
designs without turning a small experiment into a
confusing guessing game.

2
00:00:08.558 --> 00:00:12.650
We are not trying to prove which plane is best for
everyone.

3
00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:18.877
We are only comparing our two designs under the same
conditions in one practice session.

4
00:00:19.057 --> 00:00:24.959
In this practice test, both planes use one sheet of
the same size and type of paper.

5
00:00:25.139 --> 00:00:28.072
Only the fold design changes.

6
00:00:28.252 --> 00:00:30.810
You can name the designs A and B.

7
00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:35.363
Write the names on a separate record sheet, not on
the planes.

8
00:00:35.543 --> 00:00:40.403
Clear a safe indoor space and keep people out of the
flight path.

9
00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:44.280
Use the same starting line for every throw.

10
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:50.350
Each design gets three throws, so this practice
session has six throws in total.

11
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.394
Take turns between A and B rather than finishing one
design first.

12
00:00:55.574 --> 00:01:00.028
This is a small practice exercise, not a
competition.

13
00:01:00.208 --> 00:01:07.584
A surprising result is still worth writing down, and
one long flight does not tell the whole story.

14
00:01:07.764 --> 00:01:12.114
Record all six distances, including short flights.

15
00:01:12.294 --> 00:01:16.839
Do not discard a result just because it looks
disappointing.

16
00:01:17.019 --> 00:01:20.105
Keep the record with the two design names.

17
00:01:20.285 --> 00:01:27.336
Now another person can see what you actually
measured, rather than only hearing which plane you
liked.
